K-One-Kernel: jadikan mq-deadline sebagai default I/O scheduler

Pada kernel 6.6 elevator_get_default() mengembalikan NULL begitu sebuah
device punya lebih dari satu hardware queue, sehingga elevator yang
terpilih adalah "none". Device UFS termasuk kasus itu, sehingga storage
utama berjalan tanpa elevator sama sekali: tanpa read-ahead, tanpa
bounded latency, dan tanpa penggabungan request yang bersebelahan.

mq-deadline tetap valid untuk device multi-queue, jadi jadikan ia
default juga di sana. Device yang benar-benar tidak boleh memakai
elevator tetap dihormati lewat flag BLK_MQ_F_NO_SCHED_BY_DEFAULT,
dan bila mq-deadline tidak tersedia elevator_find_get() mengembalikan
NULL yang tetap berarti "none".
--- a/block/elevator.c
+++ b/block/elevator.c
@@ -564,18 +564,23 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * For single queue devices, default to using mq-deadline. If we have multiple
- * queues or mq-deadline is not available, default to "none".
+ * K-One-Kernel: default to mq-deadline for every schedulable queue.
+ *
+ * Upstream 6.6 falls back to "none" as soon as a device exposes more than
+ * one hardware queue, which is exactly the case for UFS storage. That leaves
+ * the primary storage with a no-op elevator: no read-ahead, no bounded
+ * request latency, and no merge of adjacent requests.
+ *
+ * mq-deadline is a valid elevator for multi-queue devices, so use it as the
+ * default there too. Devices that genuinely need no elevator are still
+ * honoured through BLK_MQ_F_NO_SCHED_BY_DEFAULT, and if mq-deadline is
+ * unavailable elevator_find_get() returns NULL which still means "none".
  */
 static struct elevator_type *elevator_get_default(struct request_queue *q)
 {
 	if (q->tag_set->flags & BLK_MQ_F_NO_SCHED_BY_DEFAULT)
 		return NULL;
 
-	if (q->nr_hw_queues != 1 &&
-	    !blk_mq_is_shared_tags(q->tag_set->flags))
-		return NULL;
-
 	return elevator_find_get(q, "mq-deadline");
 }
 
